<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philo of Byblos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 64–141 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Herennius Philo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> was a Greek-language writer of the Roman period, born in Byblos. His Phoenician History, which he claimed to translate from the ancient Phoenician author <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchuniathon">Sanchuniathon</a>, survives only in excerpts quoted by Eusebius of Caesarea. It presents a Phoenician account of creation and the gods, and discoveries at Ugarit have shown that some of its mythology reflects genuine older traditions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In later antiquity Byblos was celebrated as a centre of the cult of Adonis, and the writer <b>Philo of Byblos</b>, in the Roman period, claimed to translate an ancient Phoenician history by Sanchuniathon, a source on Phoenician religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Until Levantine archaeology began in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, it was known mostly from the Hebrew Bible and from later Greek writers: Lucian’s On the Syrian Goddess, fragments of the Phoenician History of <b>Philo of Byblos</b>, and Damascius.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…images and burials, from the hostile accounts of the Hebrew Bible and of Greek and Roman writers, and from the Phoenician History of <b>Philo of Byblos</b>, a Greek work of the Roman period that claimed to translate the ancient writer Sanchuniathon and survives only in quotations by Eusebius.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
